<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 6: Data Shapes                                           -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with Kenya's Test, Treat and Track malaria policy (Mbuli    -->
<!--  et al. 2016): confirmed cases rose 150% in three years while all  -->
<!--  malaria cases fell, because testing changed, not the disease.     -->
<!--  The R section then covers:                                       -->
<!--    - reshaping (flamingos; the JHU COVID-19 wide files)           -->
<!--    - appending (Kenya road accidents 2016 + 2017; fertiliser       -->
<!--      voucher lists; Kenya vs Uganda fatality definitions)          -->
<!--    - merging (road accidents + 2019 census; Homa Bay EMR systems;  -->
<!--      the IEBC voter register)                                      -->
<!--  Outstanding: the STACK question, and the TODO in ss-merging-keys. -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-data-shapes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Data Shapes</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-data-shapes">
    <p>
      In <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>, adding dimensions
      to a plot worked so nicely because every dataset was already laid out the
      way a good plotting system expects: one row per observation, and one
      column per variable.
      Add a variable, add an aesthetic mapping.
      But that only works if the data is already in the right
      <term>shape</term>.
      Real data hardly ever arrives like that.
      And the same numbers, arranged differently, can be really easy to work
      with, or almost impossible, depending on how they're laid out.
      So in this chapter, we want to convince you that the shape of a dataset
      really matters, and deserves just as much thought as the numbers inside
      it.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: The opener, on its own, told in full                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-data-shapes">
    <title>Same Column, Different Meaning</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, the problem was messy values
        inside a single file.
        This chapter is about what happens when you put files together: stacking
        them, reshaping them, joining them.
        And we want to start with a case where the files fit together perfectly,
        with the same columns and the same types, and the numbers still pointed
        the wrong way.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mbuli et al.
        (2016) looked at outpatient malaria cases reported across Kenya between
        2012 and 2015.
        One of the columns they counted went up by 150% in three years - but how
        can this happen if malaria hadn't got any worse?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-malaria-policy">
      <title>A new way of deciding who has malaria</title>

      <p>
        Kenya reports over six million malaria cases every year.
        For a long time, malaria was mostly treated <em>presumptively</em>.
        A patient arrived at a clinic with a fever, and if malaria was a
        reasonable guess, they were treated for it, without a blood test.
        That's quick, and it's often the only option where there's no
        laboratory.
        But it meant plenty of fevers caused by something else (a chest
        infection, say) were treated as malaria, and the real cause went
        unaddressed.
      </p>

      <p>
        In 2012, Kenya adopted the Test, Treat and Track (T3) policy, in line
        with the World Health Organisation.
        The idea is that every suspected case should be tested first, and only
        treated for malaria if the test is positive.
        Rapid diagnostic tests (RDTs), which don't need a laboratory, made this
        possible even in small dispensaries.
        Cases are reported through the national health information system,
        DHIS2.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-malaria-table">
      <title>Four years of reporting</title>

      <p>
        Mbuli et al.
        took the routine reports from every government health facility in the 47
        counties, from DHIS2, and totalled them by year.
        The table below is their Table 1, for outpatient cases only.
        <q>All malaria cases</q> is every case reported, whether it was
        diagnosed from symptoms alone or confirmed by a test.
        <q>Confirmed</q> is only the cases that had a positive test, so it's
        part of the first column.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-malaria-t3">
        <title>
          Outpatient malaria cases reported in Kenya's government health
          facilities, 2012 to 2015 (Mbuli et al., 2016).
        </title>

        <tabular halign="right">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Year</cell>
            <cell>All malaria cases</cell>
            <cell>Confirmed cases</cell>
            <cell>Confirmed (%)</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2012</cell>
            <cell>8,474,945</cell>
            <cell>1,969,868</cell>
            <cell>23.2</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2013</cell>
            <cell>7,871,171</cell>
            <cell>3,185,007</cell>
            <cell>40.5</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2014</cell>
            <cell>8,774,354</cell>
            <cell>4,091,494</cell>
            <cell>46.6</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2015</cell>
            <cell>6,852,506</cell>
            <cell>4,922,574</cell>
            <cell>71.8</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-malaria-two-stories">
      <title>Two stories from one table</title>

      <p>
        Suppose you only had the <q>Confirmed cases</q> column.
        Perhaps it came from the 2012 report, and the 2015 report, stacked
        together, because the columns match exactly.
        It says that confirmed malaria rose from about 2.0 million cases to
        about 4.9 million.
        On that evidence, malaria in Kenya more than doubled in three years.
        (Kenya had set itself a target of cutting the malaria burden by
        two-thirds by 2018, so that would have been alarming!)
      </p>

      <p>
        Now suppose you had the other column instead.
        All malaria cases went from 8.5 million to 6.9 million, a fall of 19%.
        That's the opposite story.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-malaria-t3">
        <caption>
          Outpatient malaria cases reported in Kenya, 2012 to 2015.
          Confirmed cases rise steeply while all malaria cases fall.
          Data from Table 1 of Mbuli et al.
          (2016).
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch6_malaria_t3.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A line chart with year from 2012 to 2015 on the horizontal axis
              and outpatient malaria cases in millions on the vertical axis.
              The line for confirmed cases rises from about 2.0 million in 2012
              to about 4.9 million in 2015.
              The line for all malaria cases stays between about 7 and 9 million
              until 2014, then drops to about 6.9 million in 2015.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        So which is right?
        They both are, and that's the point.
        What changed between 2012 and 2015 wasn't the amount of malaria, but,
        how many suspected cases got tested.
        In 2012, 23% of reported malaria cases were confirmed by a test.
        By 2015, it was 72%!
        More tests means more confirmed cases, even if the number of people with
        malaria stays the same, or falls.
        And the cases diagnosed without a test, which had been the majority,
        dropped by 70%, which is what the T3 policy was designed to do.
      </p>

      <p>
        The column was called <c>confirmed</c> in every year, so on paper it
        meant the same thing every time.
        But the amount of testing behind it was completely different, and the
        computer has no way to know that.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-malaria-lesson">
      <title>Matching columns aren't enough</title>

      <p>
        Mbuli et al.
        are careful about what they conclude.
        They say malaria cases
        <q>have been decreasing gradually over the
        period</q>, and that
        confirmation of suspected cases has increased, but that one-third of
        outpatients were still being treated without a test in 2015.
        They also note that the share of facilities reporting went from 89% to
        95% over the same years, which is another thing changing underneath the
        trend.
      </p>

      <p>
        What we take from this is about combining datasets.
        And, when you bring datasets together, there are two questions to
        answer.
        The first is the one a computer can check: do the column names match,
        and do the types match?
        The second is the one it can't check: does a row, or a number in a
        column, mean the same thing in every file?
        Has the way people were counted, tested, or recorded changed along the
        way?
        Are the definitions the same in all datasets being combined?
      </p>

      <p>
        That's what this chapter is about: the operations that bring data
        together, and the questions you need to ask before they work.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Mbuli, C.W., Waqo, E., Owiti, P.O., Tweya, H.,
        Kizito, W., Edwards, J.K., Takarinda, K.C.
        and Omondi-Ogutu (2016).
        <q>Trends of reported outpatient malaria cases to assess the Test,
        Treat and Track (T3) policy in Kenya.</q>
        <em>East African Medical Journal</em>, 93(10) (Supplement).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: the STACK question that exemplifies this              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-ch6-stack">
    <title>Practise: Appending Data</title>

    <exercise>
      <title>Appending Data</title>

      <stack source="stack/Statistics-and-Probability-Open-Question-Bank/top/STACK-for-Statistics-and-Probability/appending-data.xml" label="stk-ch6-data-shapes"/>
    </exercise>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: Applying and looking at different shapes              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-applied">
    <title>Reshaping Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In this section, we work through these shape problems in R, using a few
        different datasets (flamingos, the Kenya road accidents data, and the
        Kenya census).
        Along the way, we also look at some real examples of each problem out in
        the wild.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-long-to-wide">
      <title>Wide or Long?</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The same data can be arranged in really different ways, depending on
          who needs to use it:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Long format</term> is one row per observation, one column
                per variable.
                This is the shape that plotting and analysis tools expect.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Wide format</term> is one column per category, one row per
                group.
                This is the shape that a human reader often wants to scan.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Knowing when to use each one, and how to move between them, is a
          really useful skill when you're working with data.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-wide-vs-long">
          <caption>
            The same flamingo counts in wide and long format.
            In wide format there is one row per lake and one column per species.
            In long format there is one row per lake and species combination,
            with the species names stored as values in a column.
            <c>pivot_longer()</c> goes from wide to long, and
            <c>pivot_wider()</c> goes back.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_wide_vs_long.png" width="90%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A wide table with columns for lake, Greater Flamingo and Lesser
                Flamingo and three rows, one each for Bogoria, Elementaita and
                Nakuru.
                Beside it, a long table with columns for lake, species and n and
                six rows, one for each lake and species pair.
                Counts for Greater Flamingo are shaded blue and counts for
                Lesser Flamingo are shaded orange in both tables.
                Arrows labelled pivot_longer and pivot_wider point between the
                two tables.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-shapes-long-to-wide">
        <title>Pivoting the Flamingos Data</title>

        <p>
          <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> has been in <term>long</term> format all
          along: one row per bird, and one column per variable.
          That's why all our visualisations with <c>ggplot2</c> have worked
          without any reshaping.
          But long format isn't always what you want to <em>look</em> at, and
          reshaping between the two is a skill in its own right.
        </p>

        <p>
          Say you want a simple summary table of how many birds there are of
          each species at each lake, laid out the way someone reading a report
          would expect: lakes down the side, and species across the top.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)

        counts_long &lt;- kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
          count(lake, species)

        head(counts_long)

        #&gt;          lake          species  n
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria Greater Flamingo 10
        #&gt; 2     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo 114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo 30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita  Lesser Flamingo 38
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru Greater Flamingo 26
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo 126
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          <c>counts_long</c> is exactly the shape a computer wants: one row for
          each lake and species combination, and one column holding the count.
          But it's not the easiest shape for a person to read.
          It's much easier to compare species across lakes when the species are
          in columns instead of rows.
          <c>pivot_wider()</c> turns the <c>species</c> values into column
          names.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        counts_wide &lt;- counts_long %&gt;%
          pivot_wider(names_from = species, values_from = n)

        counts_wide

        #&gt;          lake Greater Flamingo Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria               10             114
        #&gt; 2 Elementaita               30              38
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru               26             126
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Now the table is really easy to read by eye, but it's no longer in a
          shape that <c>ggplot2</c> can plot directly.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>From wide to long</term>
        </p>

        <p>
          If we reverse the process with <c>pivot_longer()</c>,
          <c>counts_wide</c> becomes plottable again.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        counts_long &lt;- counts_wide %&gt;%
          pivot_longer(cols = -lake, names_to = "species", values_to = "n")
        counts_long

        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 3
        #&gt;   lake        species              n
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Bogoria     Greater Flamingo    10
        #&gt; 2 Bogoria     Lesser Flamingo    114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo    30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Lesser Flamingo     38
        #&gt; 5 Nakuru      Greater Flamingo    26
        #&gt; 6 Nakuru      Lesser Flamingo    126

        
ggplot(counts_long, aes(x = lake, y = n, fill = species)) + 
  geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
  labs(x = "Lake", y = "Count", fill = "Species")
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure>
          <caption>
            A bar chart with three bars comparing the number of flamingoes at
            each lake, split by species.
            Created using data in a long format.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_long_plot.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A bar chart with three bars comparing the number of flamingoes
                at each lake, split by species.
                The bar for Nakuru is the tallest, followed by Bogoria, then
                Elementaita.
                The bar for Lesser Flamingoes is taller than the bar for Greater
                Flamingoes at each lake.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          This round trip, from long to wide and back to long again, is worth
          the effort.
          Each shape serves a different reader.
          Long format serves R: <c>ggplot2</c>, <c>group_by()</c>, and joins all
          expect it.
          Wide format serves a person scanning a table by eye, or a spreadsheet
          formula that expects species to be in fixed columns.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="sec-jhu-shapes">
          <title>A Format That Grew a New Column Every Day</title>

          <p>
            In January 2020, a small team at Johns Hopkins University's Center
            for Systems Science and Engineering (CSSE) launched a public
            COVID-19 dashboard, which they described that May in a peer-reviewed
            paper (Dong, Du, and Gardner, 2020).
            Within weeks, it had become one of the most-used sources of COVID-19
            data in the world: the map that news outlets, governments, and
            researchers everywhere were pulling numbers from.
            The data behind it was published openly, and it started out
            <term>wide</term>: one row for each country or region, and one
            column for every date, holding that location's total case count so
            far on that day.
          </p>

          <p>
            <alert>A table with no final column</alert>
          </p>

          <p>
            Most wide tables have a fixed set of columns that you know in
            advance.
            This one didn't.
            Every single day the pandemic went on, the dataset needed one more
            column than the day before, for as long as anyone was tracking the
            outbreak.
            Anyone who wanted to do more than look at the day's totals (plot a
            trend, work out a weekly change, or merge in a country's population
            to calculate a rate) first had to reshape the table from wide to
            long.
            And they had to do it again every time a new day's column was added.
          </p>

          <p>
            With each column representing a date, there was no straightforward
            way to record different measures, such as confirmed cases, deaths,
            and recoveries, in the same table.
            Instead, the data were split across three separate datasets: one for
            confirmed cases, one for deaths, and one for recoveries.
            Anyone wanting to compare these measures, or calculate something
            like the case fatality rate, first had to bring the datasets
            together.
          </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-covid-wide-format">
            <caption>
              Confirmed COVID-19 cases worldwide, 22 January to 2 February 2020.
              Each row represents a country or region, while each date is stored
              as a separate column.
              As more days are added, the dataset quickly becomes very wide.
              This is one of three datasets in the original collection:
              Confirmed Cases, Deaths, and Recovered.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/covid_wide_format.png" width="60%">
              <description>
                <p>
                  A screenshot of a wide COVID-19 dataset.
                  Rows represent countries and regions, with columns for
                  location, latitude and longitude followed by daily confirmed
                  case counts from 22 January 2020 onwards.
                  The many date columns illustrate how the dataset becomes
                  increasingly wide as observations are recorded for each new
                  day.
                  This is the "Confirmed Cases" dataset; equivalent datasets
                  were provided for deaths and recovered cases.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <p>
            <alert>Reshaping it themselves, and having the ground move</alert>
          </p>

          <p>
            For a dashboard that people glanced at once a day, wide format
            worked perfectly.
            But some people needed to do more than glance at the data, so they
            reshaped it themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            On 23 March 2020, the researcher Joachim Gassen published R code
            that took JHU's wide file and reshaped it to long (one row per
            country per date), specifically so it could be merged with other
            data.
            <term>Merging</term> means bringing together information from two
            datasets by matching rows that refer to the same thing.
            For example, the JHU data contained the number of COVID-19 cases in
            each country, but not each country's population.
            By merging it with a second dataset containing population figures,
            researchers could calculate cases per 100,000 people, making
            comparisons between countries of very different sizes more
            meaningful.
          </p>

          <p>
            To merge datasets, however, they need a shared identifier, something
            that tells us which rows belong together.
            A wide table with a different date in every column has no single
            date column to match against another dataset, but a long table does.
            Gassen's code went a step further, attaching standardised
            <term>ISO3c</term> country codes to every row.
            These three-letter codes, such as GHA for Ghana and KEN for Kenya,
            allow countries to be matched across datasets even when their names
            are written differently.
          </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-covid-long-format">
            <caption>
              The same confirmed-case numbers as
              <xref ref="fig-covid-wide-format"/>, reshaped to long format: one
              row per country per date, with a standardised country code
              attached for merging.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/covid_long_format.png" width="60%">
              <description>
                <p>
                  A long-format COVID-19 dataset with one row per country per
                  date.
                  Columns include a standardised ISO3c country code, country
                  name, date, and confirmed case count, instead of one column
                  per date.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <p>
            <term>Sources:</term> Dong, E., Du, H.
            and Gardner, L.
            (2020).
            <q>An interactive web-based dashboard to track COVID-19 in real
        time.</q>
            <em>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</em>, 20(5), 533 <ndash/> 534.
            Gassen, J.
            (23 March 2020).
            <q>Tidying the John Hopkins Covid-19 data.</q> Blog post.
            Gassen, J.
            (24 March 2020).
            <q>Tidying the new Johns Hopkins Covid-19
        time-series datasets.</q>
            Blog post.
            Data repository:
            <url href="https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19" visual="github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19"/>.
          </p>
        </example>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-append">
      <title>Appending Data</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Appending is the simplest shape operation: you take two datasets that
          describe the same kind of thing, and stack them into one.
          It sounds straightforward, and when everything is consistent, it is!
          But the column names, data types, and variable definitions all need to
          match.
          When they don't, you either get an error, or (more dangerously) a
          silent expansion that looks fine until you try to use it.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-shapes-append">
        <title>Appending the 2016 and 2017 Kenya Road Accidents Data</title>

        <p>
          In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/> and
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we worked with the Kenya Road
          Accidents database for April to June 2016.
          It turns out the same Excel file has a second sheet, with data from
          2017.
          This is a natural appending problem: two datasets recording the same
          kind of information, in the same format, for different time periods.
          We want to combine them into one.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)
        library(readxl)

        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")
        kenya_accidents_2017 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx",
                                            sheet = "2017")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Before we combine anything, it's worth looking at both datasets side
          by side to see how they compare.
          Which columns do they share?
          What's different?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        head(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 13
        #&gt;   `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY       ROAD            PLACE
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI      KITUI-ITHOKWE   KITUI SCHOOL
        #&gt; 2 830             VOI             TAITA TAVETA MOMBASA-NAIROBI IKANGA
        #&gt; 3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI       MOMBASA-NAIROBI KATOLANI
        #&gt; 4 2100            ONGATA RONGAI   NAKURU       NAKURU-NAIROBI  MAASAI LODGE
        #&gt; 5 1900            MATUU           MACHAKOS     MATUU-MWINGI    KIVANI
        #&gt; 6 2130            NYANDO          HOMA BAY     KATITO-KENDU B… PAP ONDITI
        #&gt; # ... with 8 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, ...13 &lt;chr&gt;

        head(kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 15
        #&gt;   `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY  ROAD  PLACE `MV INVOLVED`
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 745             KISUMU          KISUMU  NAIR… KASA… KBS 163T/ZD …
        #&gt; 2 1430            NAROK           NAROK   NARO… NARO… KBS 518A HON…
        #&gt; 3 1515            LONDIANI        KERICHO KERI… JUBE… KTCB 472K JO…
        #&gt; 4 1600            KIMILILI        BUNGOMA BOKO… BITU… KBW 374M TOY…
        #&gt; 5 1747            NAKURU          NAKURU  PIPE… PIPE… KMDQ 433R DA…
        #&gt; 6 1830            ONGATA RONGAI   KAJIADO MAGA… CHAP… KBG 936M T/M…
        #&gt; # ... with 9 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;, AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;, Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, ...15 &lt;dttm&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The two datasets share 12 columns, with the same names and the same
          data types: <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c>, <c>COUNTY</c>, <c>GENDER</c>,
          <c>AGE</c>, and so on.
          The 2016 data has a 13th column ( <c>...13</c> ), which we found out
          in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> is just a stray note.
          The 2017 data has three extra columns that 2016 doesn't have:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <c>MV
          INVOLVED</c> (the vehicle registration number)
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <c>NAME OF
          VICTIM</c> (the name of the individual(s)
                in the incident)
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                its own stray column ( <c>...15</c> ).
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          We drop the stray columns from both before we go any further.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016$`...13` &lt;- NULL
        kenya_accidents_2017$`...15` &lt;- NULL
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          <alert>When the columns match: a clean append</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          In <c>dplyr</c>, <c>bind_rows()</c> stacks two data frames on top of
          each other: the rows of the second go underneath the rows of the
          first.
          It's the computer version of printing out both spreadsheets and
          stapling them together.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_all &lt;- bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                          kenya_accidents_2017)

        head(kenya_accidents_all, 10)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 10 × 14
        #&gt;    `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY       ROAD            PLACE
        #&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt;  1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI      KITUI-ITHOKWE   KITUI SCHOOL
        #&gt;  2 830             VOI             TAITA TAVETA MOMBASA-NAIROBI IKANGA
        #&gt;  3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI       MOMBASA-NAIROBI KATOLANI
        #&gt;  4 2100            ONGATA RONGAI   NAKURU       NAKURU-NAIROBI  MAASAI LODGE
        #&gt;  5 1900            MATUU           MACHAKOS     MATUU-MWINGI    KIVANI
        #&gt;  6 2130            NYANDO          HOMA BAY     KATITO-KENDU B… PAP ONDITI
        #&gt;  7 1200            MWEA            KIRINYAGA    MAKUTANO-MUMB… MUMAI
        #&gt;  8 1330            NAKURU          NAKURU       KENYA-UGANDA    NEAR NAKURU
        #&gt;  9 1930            KAYOLE          NAIROBI      EASTERN BYPASS  NEAR KAYOLE
        #&gt; 10 2030            THIKA           KIAMBU       NYERI ROAD      BLUE POSTS
        #&gt; # ... with 4 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, MV INVOLVED &lt;chr&gt;, NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          It works!
          Notice that the columns that only exist in the 2017 data (
          <c>MV
          INVOLVED</c> and <c>NAME OF VICTIM</c> ) are in the
          combined dataset, filled with <c>NA</c> for all the 2016 rows.
          <c>bind_rows()</c> handles this really nicely: it creates a column for
          every column in either dataset, and fills the gaps with missing
          values.
          No data is lost, and there are no errors.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is what that looks like at the join between the two years.
          The table shows six of the columns for the last three rows of the 2016
          data (rows 376 to 378) and the first three rows of the 2017 data (rows
          379 to 381).
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-bind-rows-result">
          <title>
            Six columns from the result of
            <c>bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)</c>,
            showing the last three rows from 2016 and the first three rows from
            2017.
            <c>MV INVOLVED</c> and <c>NAME OF VICTIM</c> only exist in the 2017
            data, so they are <c>NA</c> for every 2016 row.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME 24 HOURS</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>VICTIM</cell>
              <cell>Date DD/MM/YYYY</cell>
              <cell>MV INVOLVED</cell>
              <cell>NAME OF VICTIM</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>376</cell>
              <cell>2040</cell>
              <cell>KAJIADO</cell>
              <cell>P/PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-01</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>377</cell>
              <cell>1830</cell>
              <cell>NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-01</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>378</cell>
              <cell>2230</cell>
              <cell>NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>PEDESTRIAN</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-01</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>379</cell>
              <cell>745</cell>
              <cell>KISUMU</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>2017-11-06</cell>
              <cell>KBS 163T/ZD 9531 AXIOR TRAILER</cell>
              <cell>UNKNOWN</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>380</cell>
              <cell>1430</cell>
              <cell>NAROK</cell>
              <cell>PEDESTRIAN</cell>
              <cell>2017-11-06</cell>
              <cell>KBS 518A HONDA CRV</cell>
              <cell>UNKNOWN</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>381</cell>
              <cell>1515</cell>
              <cell>KERICHO</cell>
              <cell>DRIVER</cell>
              <cell>2017-11-06</cell>
              <cell>KTCB 472K JOHN DEER</cell>
              <cell>UNKNOWN</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          We can check the append worked by counting the rows from each year.
          If both years are there, we should see rows from 2016 and from 2017.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(ggplot2)
        library(lubridate)

        kenya_accidents_all %&gt;%
          mutate(year = lubridate::year(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)) %&gt;%
          count(year)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 2
        #&gt;    year     n
        #&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1  2016   378
        #&gt; 2  2017   740
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There are 378 rows from 2016 and 740 from 2017, so both years are
          there, as expected.
          This is a quick check that's worth doing after every append: make sure
          the row count is what you expected, and that the data from each source
          is actually there.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's also worth checking the columns.
          The 2016 data had 12 columns and the 2017 data had 14, but the
          combined dataset doesn't have 12 + 14 = 26 columns.
          It has 14, because most of the columns are shared, so they only appear
          once.
          The two extra columns from 2017 ( <c>MV INVOLVED</c> and
          <c>NAME OF
          VICTIM</c> ) are added to the shared set, giving
          14 in total.
          If a column appeared in one dataset but not the other, and it really
          was a different variable (and not just a naming inconsistency), you'd
          expect to see it as an extra column, filled with <c>NA</c> s for the
          dataset that didn't have it.
          So checking the number of columns after an append, and looking closely
          at any columns you didn't expect, is a quick way to catch naming
          mismatches before they cause problems later.
        </p>

        <remark>
          <title>When the column names don't match</title>

          <p>
            A clean append depends on both datasets using the same column names
            for the same variables.
            In practice, one problem could easily be different offices using
            different names for the same field, and then the files couldn't be
            combined correctly.
            We can recreate that problem on purpose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let's pretend the 2016 data had been collected with a column called
            <c>SEX</c> instead of <c>GENDER</c>.
            We can simulate that by renaming it:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- dplyr::rename(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                               "SEX" = "GENDER")
        names(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt;  [1] "TIME 24 HOURS"          "BASE/SUB BASE"         
        #&gt;  [3] "COUNTY"                 "ROAD"                  
        #&gt;  [5] "PLACE"                  "BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS"
        #&gt;  [7] "SEX"                    "AGE"                   
        #&gt;  [9] "CAUSE CODE"             "VICTIM"                
        #&gt; [11] "NO."                    "Date DD/MM/YYYY"
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            So what happens when we append now?
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 10 × 16
        #&gt;    `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY  ROAD  PLACE
        #&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt;  1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI KITU… KITU…
        #&gt;  2 830             VOI             TAITA … MOMB… IKAN…
        #&gt;  3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI  MOMB… KATO…
        #&gt; # ... with 7 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   SEX &lt;chr&gt;, AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NO. &lt;dbl&gt;, Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, MV INVOLVED &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            There's no error, but look carefully at the columns.
            <c>SEX</c> and <c>GENDER</c> both appear in the combined dataset, as
            separate columns.
            R has no way of knowing that <c>SEX</c> in the 2016 data and
            <c>GENDER</c> in the 2017 data are the same variable.
            As far as R is concerned, they're two different column names, so
            they become two different columns.
            The 2016 rows have values in <c>SEX</c> and <c>NA</c> in
            <c>GENDER</c>, and the 2017 rows have values in <c>GENDER</c> and
            <c>NA</c> in <c>SEX</c>.
            All the information is still there, but it's now split across two
            columns that should be one.
            Any analysis of sex or gender would need to bring them back together
            first.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best fix is to make the column names match before you append:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- dplyr::rename(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                               "GENDER" = "SEX")
            </code>
          </program>
        </remark>

        <p>
          <alert>When the column types don't match</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          A more subtle problem comes up when both datasets use the same column
          name, but store the values as different types.
          The date column is a good example.
          In both datasets, <c>Date DD/MM/YYYY</c> is read in as a
          <c>POSIXct</c> datetime object.
          But suppose we convert it to a simpler <c>Date</c> type in the 2016
          data as part of cleaning, like we did in
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, and leave the 2017 data as it is:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
          mutate(`Date DD/MM/YYYY` = as.Date(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`))

        class(kenya_accidents_2016$`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)
        #&gt; [1] "Date"

        class(kenya_accidents_2017$`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)
        #&gt; [1] "POSIXct" "POSIXt"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Both of these are dates, but R stores them differently.
          So what happens when we append?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          It works!
          <c>bind_rows()</c> silently converts both to <c>dttm</c> (datetime)
          format to sort out the conflict.
          That's convenient, but notice the word <q>silently</q>: R made a
          decision about your data without telling you.
          Here, that decision doesn't do any harm, but in general, it's worth
          checking the types in the combined dataset after you append, instead
          of assuming everything was sorted out correctly.
        </p>

        <p>
          Not every type mismatch gets sorted out so nicely.
          Convert <c>AGE</c> to numeric in the 2016 data with
          <c>as.numeric()</c>, like we did in
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, and leave it as character in the
          2017 data.
          We'll do this on a copy of the 2016 data, so the original stays as it
          is for later in the chapter.
          This time, you get an error when you try to append:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016_numeric &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
          mutate(AGE = as.numeric(AGE))

        class(kenya_accidents_2016_numeric$AGE)
        #&gt; [1] "numeric"

        class(kenya_accidents_2017$AGE)
        #&gt; [1] "character"

        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016_numeric, kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; Error in `bind_rows()`:
        #&gt;   ! Can't combine `..1$AGE` &lt;double&gt; and `..2$AGE` &lt;character&gt;.
        #&gt; Run `rlang::last_trace()` to see where the error occurred.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Unlike the dates, R can't decide by itself whether a number and a
          piece of text are the same thing, so instead of guessing, it refuses.
          And this error is actually really helpful: it tells you exactly which
          column is causing the problem, and what the conflict is.
          The fix is to make the types match before you append, either by
          converting <c>AGE</c> to numeric in both datasets, or by leaving it as
          character in both.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-append-lesson">
          <title>
            Appending reveals assumptions you didn't know you'd made.
          </title>

          <p>
            A clean append needs both datasets to agree on three things between
            the two data frames:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  the (exact) same column names for the variables to append.
                  Two records that look identical to a person can be completely
                  different to a computer, if the column names, spacing, or
                  exact text differ by even one character.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  the same data types for each column (e.g., both have to be
                  numeric, or factors, etc.)
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  the same definition of what a row is.
                  This is very important!
                  <xref ref="subsec-ss-shapes-definitions"/> goes into this in
                  more detail.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
            When any of those breaks, you either get an error (which at least
            tells you something's gone wrong), or a silent expansion, with extra
            columns appearing and types being quietly changed, which might not.
            The two sheets of the Kenya road accidents data pass all three,
            which is why the append works so cleanly.
            That's no accident: it's because the same data collection format was
            used in both years.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <example xml:id="subsec-ss-shapes-definitions">
          <title>Same Column Name, Different Definition</title>

          <p>
            In Kenya, the NTSA only records deaths at the scene, with no
            follow-up at hospitals to find out whether people later died of
            their injuries (Muguro et al., 2020).
          </p>

          <p>
            In Uganda, a road crash counts as fatal if someone dies at the
            scene, or within a year and a day of the crash because of their
            injuries (Nankunda and Evdorides, 2025).
          </p>

          <p>
            Someone who died at the scene would count as a road fatality in both
            countries.
            But someone who survived the crash and died in hospital six months
            later would count in Uganda, but not in Kenya.
          </p>

          <p>
            If we simply appended these datasets, we would be combining data
            collected under different definitions, even though the columns
            appeared to match perfectly.
            This could make Uganda's road fatality figures appear higher than
            Kenya's, not necessarily because its roads are more dangerous, but
            <term>because deaths are counted differently!</term>
          </p>

          <p>
            The <c>bind_rows()</c> operation would work technically, but the
            resulting data could lead us to misleading conclusions.
            This is why checking that columns have the same names and data types
            is not enough.
            We also need to understand
            <alert>what the values actually represent</alert> before combining
            datasets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now suppose we wanted to append our 2016 Kenya road crash data to
            some equivalent data from Uganda, so we could compare them.
            The two datasets might have the same column names, the same data
            types, and the same structure (a row per crash, with columns for
            time, location, victim category, and cause), so <c>bind_rows()</c>
            would run without any errors.
            But Kenya and Uganda define a road fatality differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            <term>Sources:</term> Muguro et al.
            (2020), cited in <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, on the Kenya NTSA
            fatality definition.
            Nankunda, C.
            and Evdorides, H.
            (2025).
            <q>Data Availability for Road Crash Valuation in Low- and Middle-Income
          Countries: A Case Study in Uganda.</q>
            <em>Future Transportation</em>, 5(1), 29.
            <url href="https://doi.org/10.3390/futuretransp5010029" visual="doi.org/10.3390/futuretransp5010029"/>.
          </p>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-append-malaria-t3">
          <title>Back to the Malaria Data</title>

          <p>
            At the start of this chapter, we looked at Kenya's outpatient
            malaria reports (Mbuli et al., 2016).
            Suppose the 2012 and 2015 reports arrive as separate files, and we
            want to put them together:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
          library(dplyr)

          malaria_2012 &lt;- tibble(year = 2012,
                                 all_malaria = 8474945,
                                 confirmed = 1969868)

          malaria_2015 &lt;- tibble(year = 2015,
                                 all_malaria = 6852506,
                                 confirmed = 4922574)

          bind_rows(malaria_2012, malaria_2015)
          #&gt; # A tibble: 2 x 3
          #&gt;    year all_malaria confirmed
          #&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
          #&gt; 1  2012     8474945   1969868
          #&gt; 2  2015     6852506   4922574
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            It runs without any complaint, because the names and types match.
            Nothing in the result warns us that <c>confirmed</c> more than
            doubled while <c>all_malaria</c> fell.
            The only way to see it is to ask what is behind the column, which in
            this case means checking what share of cases were tested:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
          bind_rows(malaria_2012, malaria_2015) %&gt;%
            mutate(pct_confirmed = round(confirmed / all_malaria * 100, 1))
          #&gt; # A tibble: 2 x 4
          #&gt;    year all_malaria confirmed pct_confirmed
          #&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;
          #&gt; 1  2012     8474945   1969868          23.2
          #&gt; 2  2015     6852506   4922574          71.8
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            The share of cases that were tested went from 23% to 72%.
            That's the thing that really changed, and it's a variable you have
            to go looking for, because the append itself will never flag it.
            Before you combine files, ask whether whatever sits behind a column,
            such as how many people were tested, stayed the same.
          </p>
        </example>

        <warning xml:id="insight-variable-definitions">
          <title>Same Name, Different Meaning</title>

          <p>
            When you append, software checks whether the column names and data
            types match.
            It can't check whether the data behind them was produced in
            comparable ways.
            So before you combine data from different sources, ask yourself:
            <q>are these datasets actually measuring the same reality?</q>
          </p>

          <p>
            Two columns can have exactly the same name, and still mean different
            things.
          </p>
        </warning>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-merge">
      <title>Merging: Bringing Different Information Together</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Appending stacks datasets that describe the same kind of thing.
          Merging does something different: it brings together two datasets that
          describe <em>different</em> things about the same subjects.
          The Kenya road accidents data tells us where and when each crash
          happened, and who was involved.
          When we explored the data, we saw that Nairobi had the most crashes.
          But Nairobi also has the biggest population of any county in Kenya.
          So does that mean Nairobi's roads are the most dangerous, or just that
          more people use them?
          The 2019 Kenya census tells us how many people live in each county.
          Put the two datasets together, and they can answer a question that
          neither one could answer alone:
          <em>which counties have the most
          dangerous roads for the size of their population?</em>
          And the answer, it turns out, isn't Nairobi!
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-merge-census">
        <title>Loading the census data</title>

        <p>
          The county-level data from the 2019 Kenya Population and Housing
          Census is available directly from the Kenya National Bureau of
          Statistics (KNBS) website, and also from the Humanitarian Data
          Exchange (HDX), which is where the accidents data came from too.
          <url href="https://www.knbs.or.ke/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx">Download
          the county-level Excel file</url>
          and load it into R:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- read_excel(
          "2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx"
        )

        head(kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 10
        #&gt;   `Population by Sex, Househo...` ...2  ...3  ...4  ...5  ...6  ...7  ...8  ...9  ...10
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                           &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 NA                              NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA
        #&gt; 2 NA                              Total Sex   NA    NA    Hous… NA    NA    Land… Dens…
        #&gt; 3 County                          NA    Male  Fema… Inte… Total Conv… Grou… Sq Km Pers…
        #&gt; 4 KENYA                           4756… 2354… 2401… 1524  1214… 1204… 1008… 5808… 81.8…
        #&gt; 5 MOMBASA                         1208… 6102… 5980… 30    3784… 3762… 2127  219.… 5495…
        #&gt; 6 KWALE                           8668… 4251… 4416… 18    1731… 1728… 374   8253… 105.…
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Well, this is an interesting start!
          The spreadsheet has two rows of headers: a merged header spanning
          several columns on row 2, and the actual column names on row 3.
          The data doesn't start until row 4.
          R expects exactly one header row, so it has used the first row as
          column names, and read everything else as data.
          So we skip the first three rows, and give the columns our own names:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- read_excel(
          "2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx",
          skip = 3
        )

        names(kenya_census_2019) &lt;- c("County", "Population", "Male", "Female",
                                       "Intersex", "Households",
                                       "Households_Conventional",
                                       "Households_Group_Quarters",
                                       "Land_Area_Sq_Km",
                                       "Density_Per_Sq_Km")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The last row of the file is a source note, and isn't data.
          If we flick through the data, we can see that <c>Population</c> has a
          value in every row except this one.
          So we can drop that last row by removing any row where
          <c>Population</c> is <c>NA</c>.
          The file also has a row for <c>KENYA</c> as a whole.
          That's the national total, and isn't a county, so we remove it too:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        tail(kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 10
        #&gt;   County       Population    Male  Female Intersex Households ...
        #&gt; 1 HOMA BAY        1131950  539560  592367       23     262036 ...
        #&gt; 2 MIGORI          1116436  536187  580214       35     240168 ...
        #&gt; 3 KISII           1266860  605784  661038       38     308054 ...
        #&gt; 4 NYAMIRA          605576  290907  314656       13     150669 ...
        #&gt; 5 NAIROBI CITY    4397073 2192452 2204376      245    1506888 ...
        #&gt; 6 Source: 201…         NA      NA      NA       NA         NA ...

        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- kenya_census_2019 %&gt;%
          dplyr::filter(!is.na(Population), County != "KENYA")
          </code>
        </program>

        <note>
          <p>
            Notice that the census data is in wide format: one row per county,
            with separate columns for the male and female populations.
            That's the right shape for a summary table, but as we saw earlier in
            this chapter, we might want to reshape it later if we want to plot
            or analyse the data by sex.
          </p>
        </note>

        <p>
          <alert>Telling R what to join on</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          Before we join, we need to tell R which column in each dataset holds
          the shared identifier.
          In <c>dplyr</c>, the join functions have a <c>by</c> argument for
          this.
          What happens if we forget it?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_join &lt;- dplyr::full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                           kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; Error in `full_join()`:
        #&gt;   ! `by` must be supplied when `x` and `y` have no common variables.
        #&gt;   ℹ Use `cross_join()` to perform a cross-join.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The error tells us exactly what's wrong: the two datasets don't share
          any columns with the same name, so R can't guess which columns to join
          on.
          In the accidents data, the county column is called <c>COUNTY</c>, and
          in the census, it's called <c>County</c>.
          So we tell R which columns match:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_join &lt;- dplyr::full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                           kenya_census_2019,
                                           by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          But when we look at the result, there's a problem.
          Here are the first five rows of the joined dataset:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-joined-sample">
          <title>
            First five rows of the joined dataset.
            Row 2 is <c>TAITA TAVETA</c>, which did not yet match the census
            name <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME 24 HOURS</cell>
              <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>Population</cell>
              <cell>Male</cell>
              <cell>Female</cell>
              <cell>Intersex</cell>
              <cell>Households</cell>
              <cell>Land Area (Sq Km)</cell>
              <cell>Density (per Sq Km)</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>630</cell>
              <cell>KITUI</cell>
              <cell>MAKUENI</cell>
              <cell>KITUI-ITHOKWE</cell>
              <cell>KITUI SCHOOL</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>987,653</cell>
              <cell>489,691</cell>
              <cell>497,942</cell>
              <cell>20</cell>
              <cell>244,669</cell>
              <cell>8,176.67</cell>
              <cell>120.79</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>830</cell>
              <cell>VOI</cell>
              <cell>TAITA TAVETA</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>IKANGA</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>1330</cell>
              <cell>MARIAKANI</cell>
              <cell>KILIFI</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>KATOLANI</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>1,453,787</cell>
              <cell>704,089</cell>
              <cell>749,673</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>298,472</cell>
              <cell>12,553.27</cell>
              <cell>115.81</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>2100</cell>
              <cell>ONGATA RONGAI</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>MAASAI LODGE</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>2,162,202</cell>
              <cell>1,077,272</cell>
              <cell>1,084,835</cell>
              <cell>95</cell>
              <cell>616,046</cell>
              <cell>7,504.91</cell>
              <cell>288.11</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>1900</cell>
              <cell>MATUU</cell>
              <cell>MACHAKOS</cell>
              <cell>MATUU-MWINGI</cell>
              <cell>KIVANDINI</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>1,421,932</cell>
              <cell>710,707</cell>
              <cell>711,191</cell>
              <cell>34</cell>
              <cell>402,466</cell>
              <cell>6,037.27</cell>
              <cell>235.53</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Row 2 of the joined dataset has <c>NA</c> for all the census columns,
          because <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> didn't match the census name,
          <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
          In fact, there are several counties in the accidents data that didn't
          match the census, and so have <c>NA</c> in all the census columns.
          This happened wherever the county names were recorded differently in
          the two datasets.
          We can find them by looking for crash rows where <c>Population</c> is
          <c>NA</c>:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(Population)) %&gt;%
      pull(COUNTY) %&gt;%
      unique()
    #&gt; [1] "TAITA TAVETA"    "NAIROBI"         "KERCHO"
    #&gt; [4] "MURANGA"         "MWINGI"          "ELGEYO MARAKWET"
    #&gt; [7] "MAKURU"          "NYAHURURU"        NA
    #&gt; [10] "MARAKWET"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Each of these is a county name from the accidents data that didn't
          find a match in the census.
          If we compare them with the county names in the census data, we can
          see what went wrong:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Some are spelling differences: the accidents data used
                <c>NAIROBI</c> where the census uses <c>NAIROBI CITY</c>, and
                <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> where the census uses <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Some are errors carried forward from the original data:
                <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>MAKURU</c> were identified as typos for
                <c>KERICHO</c> and <c>NAKURU</c> back in
                <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                And some are towns that were recorded as counties when they are
                not: Nyahururu is a town in Laikipia County, and Mwingi is a
                town in Kitui County.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          For every one of these mismatches, R had no way of knowing that, for
          example, <c>NAIROBI</c> and <c>NAIROBI CITY</c> are the same place.
          The join key has to match character for character.
          One extra space, one slash, or one letter is enough for R to treat two
          values as completely different.
          We still have the crash data and the population data, but without an
          identical key, we can't connect them.
          So before we can join, we need to change those county names in the
          accidents data to match the census.
        </p>

        <p>
          We should also check whether any rows have <c>NA</c> as their county.
          These are crashes where nobody recorded the county at all:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(COUNTY))
          </code>
        </program>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-na-county">
          <title>
            Rows with missing county values.
            The county can be inferred from the <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column:
            Nanyuki and Kinangop are both towns, not counties.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME</cell>
              <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
              <cell>GENDER</cell>
              <cell>AGE</cell>
              <cell>CAUSE CODE</cell>
              <cell>VICTIM</cell>
              <cell>NO.</cell>
              <cell>Date</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2205</cell>
              <cell>NANYUKI</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>KINAMBA</cell>
              <cell>NGARUA</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle overturned killing 6 victims</cell>
              <cell>4F &amp; INF</cell>
              <cell>A</cell>
              <cell>26</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>2142</cell>
              <cell>KINANGOP</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NJAMBINI OLKALAO</cell>
              <cell>KAMAGUTA</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle hit a pedal cyclist</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>33</cell>
              <cell>98</cell>
              <cell>P/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Both rows are missing a county, but neither one is really a mystery.
          The <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column tells us where each crash was reported
          from: Nanyuki is a town in Laikipia County, and Kinangop is a
          sub-county in Nyandarua County.
          So we can fill in the missing counties using <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c>, at
          the same time as fixing all the county names we found above:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
  mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
    
    # Spelling and formatting corrections to match census names
    COUNTY == "TAITA TAVETA"    ~ "TAITA/TAVETA",
    COUNTY == "NAIROBI"         ~ "NAIROBI CITY",
    COUNTY == "ELGEYO MARAKWET" ~ "ELGEYO/MARAKWET",
    COUNTY == "MARAKWET"        ~ "ELGEYO/MARAKWET",
    COUNTY == "MURANGA"         ~ "MURANG'A",
    
    # Corrections carried forward from data cleaning in Chapter 4
    COUNTY == "MAKURU"          ~ "NAKURU",
    COUNTY == "KERCHO"          ~ "KERICHO",
    
    # Towns incorrectly recorded as counties
    # Nanyuki and Nyahururu are both towns in Laikipia County
    `BASE/SUB BASE` == "NANYUKI"         ~ "LAIKIPIA",
    COUNTY == "NYAHURURU"       ~ "LAIKIPIA",
    # Mwingi is a town in Kitui County
    COUNTY == "MWINGI"          ~ "KITUI",
    # Kinangop is a sub-county in Nyandarua County
    `BASE/SUB BASE` == "KINANGOP"        ~ "NYANDARUA",
    
    # Everything else stays as is
    TRUE ~ COUNTY
  ))

  # Confirm no remaining NA counties
    kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(COUNTY)) %&gt;%
      nrow()
    #&gt; [1] 0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Now we've applied all the county name corrections, let's check that
          there are no mismatches left:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    setdiff(unique(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY),
            unique(kenya_census_2019$County))
    #&gt; character(0)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Every county name in the accidents data now has an exact match in the
          census.
          Only now does it make sense to run the join again.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can also check the other direction: which census counties don't
          have any crashes in the accidents data?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    setdiff(unique(kenya_census_2019$County),
            unique(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY))
    #&gt; [1] "TANA RIVER"    "LAMU"          "WAJIR"
    #&gt; [4] "MANDERA"       "MARSABIT"      "ISIOLO"
    #&gt; [7] "THARAKA-NITHI" "TURKANA"       "WEST POKOT"
    #&gt; [10] "SAMBURU"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We'd expect this second list.
          These are counties with no recorded fatal crashes in this three-month
          extract, which is plausible for counties that are sparsely populated
          or remote.
        </p>

        <paragraphs>
          <title>Choosing the right join: left_join</title>

          <p>
            With the county names fixed, we can run the join again.
            But we still need to think about which kind of join to use.
            A <c>full_join()</c> keeps every row from both datasets:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
      kenya_accidents_join &lt;- full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                         kenya_census_2019,
                                         by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))

      nrow(kenya_accidents_2016)
      #&gt; [1] 378

      nrow(kenya_accidents_join)
      #&gt; [1] 388
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            We've gained 10 rows!
            These are counties that appear in the census data but not in our
            incidents data, including Tana River, Lamu, and Wajir.
            The <c>full_join()</c> has included these counties, with <c>NA</c>
            values for all the accident-related columns.
            (Note that just because a county doesn't appear in our accidents
            data doesn't mean there have never been any crashes there.
            It simply means that no fatal crashes were recorded in our dataset
            during this three-month period).
            Since we're interested in the counties in our accidents data, we
            don't need to add these extra rows.
            Instead, we can use a <c>left_join()</c> to keep all the counties
            from our accidents data and bring in the corresponding census
            information.
          </p>

          <p>
            <c>left_join()</c> keeps every row from the first (i.e., left)
            dataset given in the R code (the accidents data), brings in the
            matching information from the right dataset (the census), and throws
            away any rows on the right that don't have a match on the left.
            In other words, every crash gets population data attached, and no
            new rows are added.
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
      kenya_accidents_join &lt;- left_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                         kenya_census_2019,
                                         by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))

      nrow(kenya_accidents_join)
      #&gt; [1] 378
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            We're back to 378 rows.
            The first five rows of the joined dataset are below.
            Notice that row 2, which is <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> and didn't match
            before, now shows <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>, with a full set of population
            values.
          </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-left-joined-sample">
          <title>
            First five rows after the corrected left join.
            Every row now has population data attached.
            Row 2 previously failed to match; after correcting the county name
            to <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c> it now joins successfully.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME</cell>
              <cell>BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>Population</cell>
              <cell>Male</cell>
              <cell>Female</cell>
              <cell>Density</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>630</cell>
              <cell>KITUI</cell>
              <cell>MAKUENI</cell>
              <cell>KITUI-ITHOKWE</cell>
              <cell>KITUI SCHOOL</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>987,653</cell>
              <cell>489,691</cell>
              <cell>497,942</cell>
              <cell>120.79</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>830</cell>
              <cell>VOI</cell>
              <cell>TAITA/TAVETA</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>IKANGA</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>340,671</cell>
              <cell>173,337</cell>
              <cell>167,327</cell>
              <cell>19.86</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>1330</cell>
              <cell>MARIAKANI</cell>
              <cell>KILIFI</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>KATOLANI</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>1,453,787</cell>
              <cell>704,089</cell>
              <cell>749,673</cell>
              <cell>115.81</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>2100</cell>
              <cell>ONGATA RONGAI</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>MAASAI LODGE</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>2,162,202</cell>
              <cell>1,077,272</cell>
              <cell>1,084,835</cell>
              <cell>288.11</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>1900</cell>
              <cell>MATUU</cell>
              <cell>MACHAKOS</cell>
              <cell>MATUU-MWINGI</cell>
              <cell>KIVANDINI</cell>
              <cell>...</cell>
              <cell>1,421,932</cell>
              <cell>710,707</cell>
              <cell>711,191</cell>
              <cell>235.53</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Finally, let's check that no crash rows are missing population data:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(Population)) %&gt;%
      nrow()
    #&gt; [1] 0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Every crash has been matched to its county's census data, so the join
          is done.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>What the merge makes possible</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          With population data attached to every crash, we can now answer a
          question that the accidents data could never answer on its own: how
          many crashes were there <em>per person</em> in each county?
          Raw crash counts are always going to be higher in counties with bigger
          populations, just because more people live and travel there.
          A rate adjusts for that, and shows which counties have really
          dangerous roads, as opposed to just more traffic.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(ggplot2)
        library(dplyr)

        crash_rate &lt;- kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
          group_by(COUNTY, Population) %&gt;%
          summarise(crashes = n(), .groups = "drop") %&gt;%
          mutate(crashes_per_100k = crashes / Population * 100000) %&gt;%
          arrange(desc(crashes_per_100k))

        ggplot(crash_rate,
               aes(x = reorder(COUNTY, crashes_per_100k),
                   y = crashes_per_100k)) +
          geom_col(fill = "#44575E") +
          geom_col(data = filter(crash_rate, COUNTY == "NAIROBI CITY"),
                   fill = "#F37021") +
          coord_flip() +
          labs(
            title = "Fatal road crashes per 100,000 people by county",
            subtitle = "Kenya, April–June 2016. Nairobi highlighted in orange.",
            x = NULL,
            y = "Crashes per 100,000 population"
          ) +
          theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch6-crash-rate">
          <caption>
            Fatal road crashes per 100,000 population by county, Kenya, April to
            June 2016.
            Nairobi City is highlighted in orange.
            Makueni leads by a substantial margin, followed by Embu.
            Nairobi, which had the most crashes in raw terms, ranks third.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_crash_rate.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A horizontal bar chart with county names on the y-axis and
                crashes per 100,000 population on the x-axis, ordered from
                highest to lowest rate.
                Makueni has the longest bar at around 2.6 crashes per 100,000,
                followed by Embu at around 1.6.
                Nairobi City is shown in orange and ranks third, just behind
                Embu, at around 1.6.
                Nakuru and Kiambu follow closely.
                The bars shorten progressively down to Nandi at the bottom with
                fewer than 0.2 crashes per 100,000.
                Counties with small populations but meaningful crash counts
                appear higher in the ranking than they would in a raw count
                chart.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          This chart tells a really different story from the raw crash counts.
          Nairobi had the most crashes in absolute terms (71 in this three-month
          period), so it would top any ranking based on counts alone.
          Makueni had 26 crashes, and Embu had just 10.
          In a chart of raw counts, Embu would barely show up.
          But Nairobi has a population of over 4.4 million people, Makueni has
          just under one million, and Embu has just over 600,000.
          Once we take population into account, Embu's 10 crashes work out as a
          slightly higher rate per person than Nairobi's 71 (both are around 1.6
          per 100,000, with Embu just ahead).
          And Makueni's 26 crashes, in a much smaller population than Nairobi's,
          give the highest rate of all, at around 2.6 per 100,000.
        </p>

        <p>
          And this really matters for policy.
          If a government decided where to invest in road safety based on raw
          crash counts, it would prioritise Nairobi, Nakuru, and Kiambu (the
          most populous counties, with the most crashes).
          A government working from crash rates would prioritise Makueni and
          Embu too.
          The ranking changes because the question changes, from
          <em>where do
          the most crashes happen?</em> to
          <em>where are people most at
          risk?</em> And the only reason
          we can ask that second question is because we merged in the population
          data.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-merge-lesson">
          <title>A join is only as good as its key</title>

          <p>
            This whole merge depended on the county names matching perfectly
            between the two datasets.
            A mismatch of even one character ( <c>NAIROBI</c> and
            <c>NAIROBI
            CITY</c>, or <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> and
            <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c> ) silently drops that county from the joined
            result.
            Without a matching key, R has no way to connect them.
            Checking the quality of your join is what tells a merge that worked
            apart from a merge that only looked like it worked.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Kenya National Bureau of Statistics (2019).
          <em>2019 Kenya Population and Housing Census, Volume I:
      Population by County and Sub-County.</em>
          Nairobi: KNBS.
          Available at:
          <url href="https://www.knbs.or.ke/2019-kenya-population-and-housing-census-results/"/>.
          Kenya Road Accidents database: openAFRICA / Humanitarian Data
          Exchange,
          <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-merging-keys">
        <title>Merging and the Idea of a Key</title>

        <p>
          Every operation that merges tables relies on the same quiet
          assumption: that somewhere in your data, there's a column that
          reliably means <q>this same real-world thing</q>, everywhere it
          appears.
          That column is called a <term>key</term>.
          In our join of the accidents and the census, the key was the county
          name, and it only worked once the names matched in both tables.
          A good key has to be there in every table you want to link, and it has
          to identify the same thing in each one.
          So what happens when there isn't a key at all?
          Here's an example from Kenya's health system.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-homa-bay-keys">
          <title>The Homa Bay Health System</title>

          <p>
            Homa Bay County, on the shore of Lake Victoria, has a high HIV
            burden and a well-established HIV care programme.
            Like health facilities across much of Kenya, its county referral
            hospital runs on electronic medical record (EMR) systems.
            A 2025 study of Homa Bay's digital health landscape found that this
            one hospital was running seven of them, side by side!
            Three are built for HIV care, and those three share a patient
            identifier.
            The other four cover general outpatient care, inpatient stays, the
            laboratory, and faith-based providers.
            They don't connect to the HIV systems, and they don't reliably
            connect to each other either.
          </p>

          <p>
            To see why that matters, let's picture a patient.
            She's hypothetical, but based on what the study found.
            She's HIV-positive and pregnant, so over a few months she ends up in
            three different systems, and each one gives her its own number:
          </p>

          <table xml:id="table-ch6-homa-bay-patient">
            <title>
              One hypothetical patient, recorded in three systems.
              The numbers are made up.
            </title>

            <tabular halign="left">
              <col halign="left"/>
              <col halign="left"/>
              <col halign="left"/>
              <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>System</cell>
                <cell>Her patient number</cell>
                <cell>What it records</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>HIV care</cell>
                <cell>HIV-0412</cell>
                <cell>HIV treatment</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Antenatal clinic</cell>
                <cell>ANC-7731</cell>
                <cell>Pregnancy visits</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Inpatient ward</cell>
                <cell>IP-20958</cell>
                <cell>Hospital admission</cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </table>

          <p>
            It's one woman, but three numbers.
            This isn't a stretch.
            The study's interviews describe exactly this pattern, with people
            <q>assigned multiple numbers for different purposes, i.e., at the
            inpatient, outpatient and antenatal clinics.</q>
            In most of the county's systems, patient numbers were created by
            hand, and in 97% of facilities they were allocated by hand at the
            patient's first visit.
            Nothing linked a person across every system they might use.
          </p>

          <p>
            And that's the point - there is no key!
            No column anywhere in this table, or in the hospital, means
            <q>this same person</q> across all three systems.
            (Within HIV care there is one, and it works fine.
            It just stops at the edge of HIV care.) A computer can't tell that
            these three rows belong to the same woman, so we can't
            <term>join</term> or <term>merge</term> them.
            If we stack the records together anyway, one of two things goes
            wrong:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  She's counted three times, which inflates the numbers reported
                  to the county or the ministry at the end of the month.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  Or a clinician treating her for a complication in her
                  pregnancy can't see that she's on HIV treatment, even though
                  that information is recorded, correctly, in the system next
                  door.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            <term>Sources:</term> Chepkirui, M., Dellicour, S., Musuva, R., et
            al.
            (2025).
            Missed opportunities for digital health data use in healthcare
            decision-making: A cross-sectional digital health landscape
            assessment in Homa Bay county, Kenya.
            <foreign>PLOS Digital Health</foreign>, 4(6), e0000870.
            https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000870
          </p>
        </example>

        <insight xml:id="insight-homa-bay-no-key">
          <title>You Cannot Join What Was Never Given a Shared Key</title>

          <p>
            Merging or reshaping assumes there's already a key linking your
            tables together.
            When separate systems are built independently, without agreeing in
            advance how a person, place, or thing will be identified across all
            of them, no amount of clever code can recover that link later.
            The key has to be designed in before the data is collected.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-data-shapes">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-reshape-census-data">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The census data we loaded in <xref ref="ss-merge-census"/> has
          separate columns for the <c>Male</c> and <c>Female</c> populations.
          Use <c>pivot_longer()</c> to reshape it so that each county appears
          twice, once for the male population and once for the female
          population, with a new column called <c>sex</c>, and a column called
          <c>population</c> holding the count.
          Then use <c>ggplot2</c> to plot the male and female populations side
          by side for each county, with a different colour for each sex.
          Which counties have the most unequal sex distributions?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-malaria-definition">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-intro-data-shapes"/> showed that matching column names
          and types isn't enough to make two datasets comparable.
          Suppose you're setting up a national reporting form, and you know that
          years from now someone will append the reports from different years.
          Suggest one extra column you would ask every facility to include, or
          one thing you would write down about how the data was collected, that
          would let a future analyst spot a change like the rise in testing
          described in <xref ref="ss-malaria-two-stories"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-flamingo-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="ss-shapes-long-to-wide"/> as a model, build a wide
          table of the mean <c>body_mass_g</c>, with <c>lake</c> as rows and
          <c>species</c> as columns, instead of counts.
          Which version, this one or the <c>counts_wide</c> table of counts,
          would you send to someone who wanted to read it in an email?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-gapminder-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>gapminder</c> data from
          <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> is in long format:
          one row per country per year.
          Use <c>pivot_wider()</c> to build a table with one row per country,
          and one column per year, holding <c>lifeExp</c>.
          Now try plotting directly from this wide version, using the same
          <c>ggplot2</c> code from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d"/>.
          What breaks, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>When a Join Gets It Wrong: Kenya's Voter Register</title>

      <stack source="stack/Statistics-and-Probability-Open-Question-Bank/top/STACK-for-Statistics-and-Probability/When-a-Join-Gets-It-Wrong-Kenya-s-Voter-Register.xml" label="stk-ch6-joining-q"/>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>